    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d214">

                <pb facs="0245" n="233" sameAs="frus:frus1945v04/pg233"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.60/5–2845</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by Fleet Admiral William D. Leahy, Chief of Staff to the Commander-in-Chief
                        of the Army and Navy, to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-05-28">28 May 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The following message from Premier J. V. Stalin to the President, dated May 27,
                    1945, is forwarded to you for the preparation of a draft reply:<anchor n="69"
                        xml:id="d214-f1"/>
                    <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“More than eight months have elapsed since Romania and Bulgaria have
                            broken off with Hitlerite Germany, have concluded armistice with the
                            Allied states and have joined the war on the side of the Allies against
                            Germany having detailed their armed forces for this purpose. By this
                            they have made a contribution to the cause of defeat of Hitlerism and
                            for the victorious completion of victory in Europe. During this time the
                            Governments of Bulgaria and Romania have proven in reality their
                            readiness to cooperate with the United Nations. In connection with this
                            the Soviet Government considers it right and timely to reestablish right
                            now diplomatic relations with Romania and Bulgaria and to exchange
                            envoys.</p>
                        <p>Simultaneously the Soviet Government considers it practicable to
                            reestablish diplomatic relations also with Finland which is fulfilling
                            the conditions of the armistice agreement and which has taken the road
                            of strengthening of diplomatic beginnings.<anchor n="70"
                                xml:id="d214-f2"/> It seems to me <pb facs="0246" n="234"
                                xml:id="pg234"/> possible, after a certain amount of time, to take a
                            similar decision in respect to Hungary.<anchor n="71" xml:id="d214-f3"/>
                        </p>
                        <p>Simultaneously I am sending a similar message to Mr. Churchill.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">William D. Leahy</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d214-f1" n="69" xml:id="d214-f1-content">
                <p>For further exchange of messages between President Truman and Marsha) Stalin on
                    June 7, 9, 19, and 23 regarding recognition of Finland, Rumania, Bulgaria, and
                    Hungary, see <ref target="frus1945Berlinv01#pg_358">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Conference of Berlin</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Potsdam</hi>) vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 358</ref>,
                    footnote 5, and <ref target="frus1945Berlinv01#pg_182">pp. 182</ref> and <ref
                        target="frus1945Berlinv01#pg_387">387</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d214-f2" n="70" xml:id="d214-f2-content">
                <p>For documentation respecting Finnish-Soviet relations at this time, see <ref
                        target="#pg598">pp. 598</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d214-f3" n="71" xml:id="d214-f3-content">
                <p>For documentation on the subject of the reestablishment of diplomatic relations
                    with Hungary, see <ref target="#pg798">pp. 798</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
